00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:56.980
So GAGE is a nine year mixed methods

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.410
longitudinal research program
exploring the gendered

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.543
experience of adolescents
10 to 19 years globally.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:09.030
We're following around
18,000 adolescents close

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:14.030
to 20,000 boys and girls in
sort of seven focal countries.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.700
So Ethiopia and Rwanda,
Nepal and Bangladesh

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.853
and then Palestine, Lebanon and Jordan.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.820
Ultimately GAGE is interested

00:05:24.335 --> 00:05:27.220
in exploring what works
for whom, where and why.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.040
So we can better support adolescent girls

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.613
and boys in the future.

00:05:33.596 --> 00:05:36.510
The GAGE I think in particular

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.650
is focused on the most
vulnerable adolescents.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:42.190
So this includes working with
refugee populations, but also

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:45.650
over sampling adolescent
girls who experienced child

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:47.710
marriage and adolescents with disability.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:49.740
So we can actually kind of quantitatively

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.150
say something about these groups.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:55.730
I should note that GAGE is funded by SCDO

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:59.770
in the UK and led by the
overseas development Institute

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:03.553
in the UK and Jennifer and
I lead on the quantitative.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:05.293
Next slide

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:11.260
So GAGE is guided by a,

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:12.810
what we call the 3Cs

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:13.940
conceptual framework.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:17.500
So capabilities, context,
and change pathways.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:22.500
This has been really important
in ensuring that these very

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.840
varied research streams
both in terms of context

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.360
we're working in programs,
we're evaluating partnerships

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.170
et cetera, sort of map
onto some overall framework

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:37.330
that helps speak more
broadly to not only what

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.700
how adolescents transition
from young adolescents

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.790
to adulthood, but also
how context interacts

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.790
with that without transition.

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.100
Our conceptual framework
is built around six core

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.430
capability domains that
you see listed here.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:56.430
This draws on a marquee
ascends work who argued that to

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.410
achieve a dignified life people

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.830
need a sort of core set of
entitlements for freedoms

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.840
which he called capabilities

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:06.030
and GAGE will identified
these six as capability areas

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.670
that are particularly
salient for adolescents.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.570
And as I mentioned on the
previous slide, we're gonna

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:14.560
focus sort of dive deeper
into three of them today.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.300
I think given both time
constraints and given where

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.960
we sort of see the most
differences between Jordan

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:24.847
and Bangladesh, as well
as where we actually see

00:07:24.847 --> 00:07:26.150
some similarities.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:30.490
This framework recognizes
that adolescents context

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.700
at the household community
and national level

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.400
are gonna shape outcomes.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.370
And I think that's gonna
be really highlighted

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:37.890
