00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:06.890
that's happening both in Jordan
and in the Rohingya camps.

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:09.030
And then I'll do a deep dive sort of

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:12.610
into psychosocial mental health outcomes

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:15.163
and exposure to violence, pre migration.

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:20.340
So first sort of starting
with education and learning.

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:23.280
I wanted to start on a
positive note, which is that

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:25.950
you know, adolescent
aspirations are quite high

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:28.230
in both of these contexts.

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:32.200
So when both Jordan and
Bangladesh, we see, you know

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:35.810
Jordan 80, over 80% of
adolescents wanna complete

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:38.270
at least some secondary
school and in Bangladesh

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:41.020
and our sample over 90% of
adolescents want to complete

00:28:41.020 --> 00:28:43.350
at least some secondary school,

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:47.790
but there's you know,
variation across populations

00:28:47.790 --> 00:28:49.340
even within the various context.

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:52.310
So in Jordan, you know, the
lowest level of aspirations

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:55.440
for secondary school is 60%
of adolescents living in these

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:59.890
in ITS in the tented communities

00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:02.713
versus 93% of Jordanian girls.

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:07.150
And then really where the
stark difference comes in

00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:11.690
is aspirations for at
least some university.

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:13.740
So, whereas in Jordan, you know

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:16.280
70% of adolescents want to complete

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:18.273
at least some university in Bangladesh.

00:29:19.233 --> 00:29:20.540
We see in this sample

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:23.420
in Cox's Bazar among the Bangladeshians

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:28.050
and Rohingya adolescents
only about 45% aspire

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.700
to at least some university,
but this is, you know

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:33.660
driven by wide disparities
and in populations.

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:37.640
So in particular, Rohingya
girls have very low aspirations

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:39.770
for university attendance

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:41.780
which is likely partially reflective

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:45.070
of like the realities of what
will be possible for them.

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.330
And as I'll be discussing sort of access

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:50.663
to education is basically
non-existent for that population.

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:56.243
The other thing I wanted
to point out is that,

00:29:58.190 --> 00:30:01.270
well actually I'll get
to that the next slide.

00:30:01.270 --> 00:30:03.140
So then I also wanted to point out that

00:30:03.140 --> 00:30:06.080
you know, parental aspirations largely

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:09.570
reflect the adolescent
aspirations themselves

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:12.630
female caregivers, over
95% in both contexts

00:30:12.630 --> 00:30:16.170
want their child to attend at
least some secondary school.

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:18.570
And then for university attendance

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:21.020
sort of reflecting what
we saw among adolescents,

00:30:22.880 --> 00:30:24.900
adolescents, you know, 88%

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:28.720
of parents are supportive
of a university education

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:33.240
in Jordan versus 32% of
parents in Bangladesh.

00:30:33.240 --> 00:30:35.850
And again, why disparities between sort

00:30:35.850 --> 00:30:38.350
of the Bangladeshi population
and the host communities

00:30:38.350 --> 00:30:41.330
versus the Rohingya
population where, you know

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:44.330
only 15% of Rohingya parents are aspiring

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:46.063
for some university education.

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:48.680
I do wanna note that actually

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:52.600
even comparing the Bangladeshi population

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:54.720
in these host communities
to other contexts

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.420
in Bangladesh we also do work

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:00.440
in the slums around
Dhaka and in Chittagong

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:05.160
and select among school going
adolescents and aspirations.

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:08.120
And those contexts are much
higher for universities.

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:12.950
So in urban slums, about 71%
of female caregivers aspire

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:15.120
for university education

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:17.770
for their children and should have gone

00:31:17.770 --> 00:31:21.400
to my new school going
adolescence it's about 93%.

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:22.270
So this also points

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:24.000
to the host communities that we're working

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:27.303
with in Cox's Bazar are also
relatively more vulnerable.

00:31:30.367 --> 00:31:34.264
So I mentioned that,
you know, the disparity

00:31:34.264 --> 00:31:37.500
in aspirations comes from
educational realities

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:41.991
and this points to access
to schooling for growth

00:31:41.991 --> 00:31:45.130
Rohingya is well, formal
schooling is non-existent.

00:31:45.130 --> 00:31:48.130
The Rohingya are restricted to camps

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:51.660
and are not allowed to
attend sort of local schools.

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.140
So the schooling that
they do have access to

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:56.830
are these informal learning centers

00:31:56.830 --> 00:31:58.880
or temporary learning
centers that are set up

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:01.120
within the camps, which
we'll talk a bit about,

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:02.660
more about in a moment,

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:05.320
but they really cater
to younger adolescents

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:08.700
and children and the very
basic level education.

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:13.030
And then more generally
in these populations

00:32:13.030 --> 00:32:15.370
restrictive gender norms prevent girls

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:18.610
from attending school once
they reach menstruation

00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:22.300
and beyond, and you know, similar patterns

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:23.670
in Jordan in terms

00:32:23.670 --> 00:32:27.180
of girls facing exclusion due
to restrictive gender norms.

00:32:27.180 --> 00:32:28.880
But the picture in terms of access

00:32:30.003 --> 00:32:34.980
to education for refugees
is a bit more positive.

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:38.670
So while initially Syrian
refugees faced some rates

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:40.730
of from education

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:45.730
Jordan introduced a
nationwide double shift system

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:47.900
that's increased access

00:32:47.900 --> 00:32:51.980
to formal education among
the Syrian refugees.

00:32:51.980 --> 00:32:56.700
And then they also have this
Makani non-formal education

00:32:56.700 --> 00:33:00.060
platform that exists in
the camps, host communities

00:33:01.732 --> 00:33:04.553
and tented settlements
that provide education.

00:33:08.400 --> 00:33:10.830
So just to give a little bit more context

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:15.713
on these education programs
that exist in both contexts.

00:33:17.180 --> 00:33:20.870
In Jordan this non-formal education

00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:22.100
programming that's available

00:33:22.100 --> 00:33:24.723
it's called Makani, which
translates to my space.

00:33:27.476 --> 00:33:28.430
And it's a UNICEF program.

00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:32.067
That's reached over a hundred
thousand vulnerable refugees

00:33:32.067 --> 00:33:33.370
and host community children

00:33:33.370 --> 00:33:36.300
in both host communities
and refugee camps.

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:38.483
And they have about 150 centers.

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:43.294
It's a comprehensive approach.

00:33:43.294 --> 00:33:47.837
So part of the Makani
programming is around education

00:33:47.837 --> 00:33:52.060
but it also is a well-rounded
approach providing

00:33:52.060 --> 00:33:56.950
child protection services,
childhood development.

00:33:56.950 --> 00:33:59.160
They wanna provide life skills

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:03.960
training and innovation labs
for adolescents and youth

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:07.070
as well as integrating health

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:10.710
and nutrition programming
through wash services.

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:14.160
And so as a whole, the Makani
programs aim to promote

00:34:14.160 --> 00:34:17.590
and contribute to
children's youth and parents

00:34:17.590 --> 00:34:19.920
full development and wellbeing sort of

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:21.530
across the board of, you know, physical

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:25.110
cognitive and socio-emotional wellbeing.

00:34:25.110 --> 00:34:30.110
And so it's an integrative
program to improve wellbeing

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:32.493
among adolescents.

00:34:33.990 --> 00:34:37.430
In Bangladesh, the main access

00:34:37.430 --> 00:34:40.150
to education for the
Rohingya adolescents living

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:43.396
in the camps are these temporary
learning centers that have

00:34:43.396 --> 00:34:45.927
are set up by UNICEF
and save the children.

00:34:45.927 --> 00:34:49.320
And in partnership with the
government of Bangladesh

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:52.550
and temporary learning
centers are much more focused

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:55.380
on trying to provide learning progression

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:57.870
for children and adolescents

00:34:57.870 --> 00:35:02.429
but very much focused on lower level, like

00:35:02.429 --> 00:35:05.840
young children with the
learning and teaching

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:07.970
focusing on like learning
to read and letters.

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:09.830
And so, you know, our experience

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:13.030
with older adolescents through
qualitative interviews is

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:15.160
that they find the programs sort of boring

00:35:16.634 --> 00:35:18.173
and like too low level.

00:35:20.330 --> 00:35:24.180
And there was some discussion

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:26.900
about expanding these
temporary learning centers

00:35:28.089 --> 00:35:30.460
to provide the Burmese curriculum

00:35:30.460 --> 00:35:33.710
for the Rohingya
adolescents in Bangladesh.

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:37.870
And that was supposed to be
expanded in 2020, however

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:42.430
that was put on hold due
to the onset of COVID.

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:44.510
And in fact these
temporary learning centers

00:35:44.510 --> 00:35:46.190
have been completely closed

00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:49.670
since last year in April.

00:35:49.670 --> 00:35:52.860
And so access to education is, again

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:55.723
essentially non-existent for adolescents.

00:35:56.980 --> 00:35:59.560
So around both of these programs

00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:04.560
we have done some work sort
of evaluating the impact

00:36:04.680 --> 00:36:07.380
of these learning opportunities

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:10.470
and programming on adolescent outcomes.

00:36:10.470 --> 00:36:11.480
And at this point

00:36:12.333 --> 00:36:13.860
given that we just have,
you know, baseline data

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:17.870
we're comparing outcomes,
according to Makani participation

00:36:17.870 --> 00:36:20.343
or the temporary learning
center participation.

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:25.370
And then we control for
factors that are relevant

00:36:25.370 --> 00:36:27.250
to selection into participation.

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:31.470
So we're controlling for
age, sex and nationality

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:34.431
which had been found
in other literature to

00:36:34.431 --> 00:36:35.840
be important drivers for participation.

00:36:35.840 --> 00:36:37.760
And then in addition, we control

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:42.210
for household resources
through asset indices

00:36:42.210 --> 00:36:44.340
whether the household had is literate

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:46.970
whether it's a female headed
household and the location.

00:36:46.970 --> 00:36:51.130
And so in Jordan, that's in
a camp its or host community

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:53.040
and in Bangladesh, that's comparing

00:36:54.245 --> 00:36:57.140
we're focused in the camps solely

00:36:57.140 --> 00:36:58.607
for the temporary learning centers.

00:36:58.607 --> 00:37:01.840
And so that's comparing
sort of techno to Uchida

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:04.693
which are the two puzzle is
where the camps are located.

00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:09.850
And then we estimate when
we are doing this analysis

00:37:09.850 --> 00:37:11.760
we also separate out our adolescents

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:15.462
into the two age cohorts,
10 to 14 year olds

00:37:15.462 --> 00:37:18.220
and 15 to 17 year olds,
because both programs focus

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:22.190
on sort of adolescents
under the age of 15.

00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:25.500
And most of Makani participants
are under the age of 10.

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:27.690
And then as I mentioned already

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:30.303
the TLC participation
is younger adolescents.

00:37:33.330 --> 00:37:35.610
So first I just wanna talk a little bit

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:37.370
on access to education.

00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:39.370
And I have a couple of figures here

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:43.520
which is estimated using the Jordan data

00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:47.430
looking at the impact of Makani
and with the two figures.

00:37:47.430 --> 00:37:50.710
The first is for the young
cohort, 10 to 14 year old

00:37:51.634 --> 00:37:53.160
looking through a range
of education outcomes

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:55.180
including enrollment in formal

00:37:55.180 --> 00:37:59.614
and non-formal schooling
as well as test scores

00:37:59.614 --> 00:38:01.260
focusing a little bit on that scores.

00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:03.480
And then the second figure
is for the older cohort

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:05.250
15 to 18 year olds.

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:06.890
And one of the things
I just wanna highlight

00:38:06.890 --> 00:38:08.800
what you can see is that, you know

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:11.410
adolescents who are enrolled in Makani

00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:13.521
or have ever been enrolled

00:38:13.521 --> 00:38:15.390
in Makani are more likely
to be currently enrolled

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:17.300
in schooling and non-formal schooling and

00:38:17.300 --> 00:38:19.500
that they tend to have better test scores

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:21.840
but that these seem to
be especially manifesting

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:25.600
for the older adolescents,
the 15 to 18 year olds, even

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:27.440
though they may not currently be enrolled

00:38:27.440 --> 00:38:28.940
in the Makani sort of pointing

00:38:29.907 --> 00:38:33.210
toward the supportive
role that Makani may have

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:36.670
played when they were
younger to carry them forward

00:38:36.670 --> 00:38:39.653
for better outcomes in later adolescence.

00:38:40.670 --> 00:38:45.280
And then we don't have as
wide of a range of outcomes

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:51.750
for the Rohingya camps, but
the temporary learning centers

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:53.630
also increase the likelihood of enrollment

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:57.400
in non-formal education by
about 64 percentage points.

00:38:57.400 --> 00:39:00.530
Just to note that the temporary
learning centers account

00:39:00.530 --> 00:39:05.530
for about 50% of the
enrollment in education,

00:39:07.290 --> 00:39:09.760
sorry about 50% of
adolescents are enrolled

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:11.470
in the temporary learning centers

00:39:11.470 --> 00:39:12.630
for the non formal education.

00:39:12.630 --> 00:39:15.281
So a lot of this is
driven by the enrollment

00:39:15.281 --> 00:39:17.113
and the non-formal education centers.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:26.960
So now, you know, turning to
violence and here I'm gonna

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:30.880
talk a bit about more like
daily violence that adolescents

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:34.620
are facing in their lives.

00:39:34.620 --> 00:39:36.249
- Sorry, just one question,

00:39:36.249 --> 00:39:37.810
one question from the chat

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:40.258
before you go on, it's
just the question is do

00:39:40.258 --> 00:39:41.240
the temporary learning
centers include child-friendly

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:43.403
spaces as well.

00:39:44.270 --> 00:39:46.053
How exactly are they set up?

00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:51.310
- Yeah, so they do have

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:54.293
child friendly spaces.

00:39:55.564 --> 00:39:59.030
I will have to check specifically

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:00.950
on how those are structured and perhaps

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:02.940
when Sarah takes back over

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:05.840
in the second half and I can
come back to respond to that.

00:40:08.291 --> 00:40:09.124
Thanks.

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:13.430
So in Jordan sort of day to day

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:19.630
experience of violence
in terms of reporting

00:40:19.630 --> 00:40:22.620
from adolescents focuses
around sexual harassment

00:40:22.620 --> 00:40:25.410
by adolescent girls as
is commonly reported

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:28.990
in the qualitative work
there's discussion of boys

00:40:28.990 --> 00:40:32.020
and young men tend to stand
around outside girls schools

00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:34.010
as they leave and follow them home.

00:40:34.010 --> 00:40:36.073
And so this is a common occurrence.

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:40.220
Nearly half of the adolescents
are reporting violence

00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:42.850
at home, and this is potentially, you know

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:45.210
violent discipline from parents

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:47.193
but this is much more heavily.

00:40:48.950 --> 00:40:51.380
A higher burden is carried by boys

00:40:51.380 --> 00:40:53.790
with 42% of boys mothers admitting

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:55.260
to using violent discipline compared

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:59.363
to 31% of girls mothers.

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:03.880
And we have a quote here
from a Syrian mother talking

00:41:03.880 --> 00:41:06.020
about how she does not beat her daughter

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:07.940
because this will affect
her but when I beat my son,

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:09.110
he will be better.

00:41:09.110 --> 00:41:10.340
That's sort of this few point

00:41:10.340 --> 00:41:14.610
of variance and discipline
between male and female children.

00:41:14.610 --> 00:41:17.270
And then bullying is common.

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:22.270
So 46% of 38% of girls report experiencing

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:25.340
peer violence which could include

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:30.007
like physical violence as well as needed.

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:38.010
The picture is really very
similar in Bangladesh where,

00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:42.610
you know both camp and host communities

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:46.410
adolescent girls are
reporting sexual harassment

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:47.983
and gender based violence.

00:41:49.422 --> 00:41:52.810
You know, 15% of married girls report

00:41:52.810 --> 00:41:57.110
having experienced some form
of gender based violence.

00:41:57.110 --> 00:42:00.770
And then again, peer violence
and bullying is common

00:42:00.770 --> 00:42:03.950
with 45% of adolescents
experiencing peer violence.

00:42:03.950 --> 00:42:06.970
And this is across both
camp and host communities.

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:09.210
So I would note that peer violence

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:10.460
is more commonly reported

00:42:10.460 --> 00:42:13.550
in host communities in
this context where 54%

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:18.090
of adolescents reporting some form

00:42:18.090 --> 00:42:21.953
of peer violence in host
communities versus 30% camps.

00:42:25.050 --> 00:42:30.050
And one of the big challenge
in term of violence comes

00:42:32.157 --> 00:42:35.740
with early marriage
and married adolescents

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:38.630
which we've already talked
a little bit about earlier

00:42:38.630 --> 00:42:42.270
in the presentation, but just to reiterate

00:42:43.805 --> 00:42:48.110
in Jordan 18% of the 15 to
17 year olds in the sample

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:51.140
almost entirely Syrian
were already married.

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:54.300
And in Bangladesh, the
share is a little lower,

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:57.160
but we found in our own sample
among 15 to 17 year old girls

00:42:57.160 --> 00:43:00.610
about 12% of the females
were already married

00:43:00.610 --> 00:43:04.220
and among married
adolescents in both Jordan

00:43:04.220 --> 00:43:08.243
and Bangladesh among the
Rohingya refugee camps.

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:13.930
It qualitative work very
strongly presented evidence

00:43:13.930 --> 00:43:15.230
of gender based violence.

00:43:15.230 --> 00:43:17.910
And so I've chosen a selection

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:21.213
of quotes here from both communities.

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:23.660
These two quotes here, you know

00:43:23.660 --> 00:43:25.160
girls are abused by their husbands

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:27.523
are both in Jordan context.

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:30.570
You know, he used to pour water in my ears

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:33.750
because these things don't
leave any marks on the outside

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:37.630
in the Rohingya camps, IPV
is happening within marriages

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:41.590
but it's very well known
even to unknown girls.

00:43:41.590 --> 00:43:42.720
And so this is a quote

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:46.840
from a 16 year old
unmarried girl who says she

00:43:46.840 --> 00:43:50.330
has heard a lot about
this from many persons

00:43:50.330 --> 00:43:53.580
when wives don't listen to
their husbands, they're beaten.

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:55.470
And, you know, just to point out

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:57.430
that the solutions to these

00:43:59.968 --> 00:44:02.920
are not well supported.

00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:04.710
And so in the Rohingya
camps, there was a lot

00:44:04.710 --> 00:44:07.260
of reporting that there's
no one reliable to report to

00:44:07.260 --> 00:44:09.580
or they aren't listened
to when they do report.

00:44:09.580 --> 00:44:11.960
And in Jordan, you know

00:44:11.960 --> 00:44:15.193
one of the main outcomes is
divorced in these settings.

00:44:21.158 --> 00:44:23.408
So in turning mental health

00:44:24.730 --> 00:44:27.350
and then we'll kind of do a deeper dive

00:44:27.350 --> 00:44:29.483
in our survey and what we focus on,

00:44:29.483 --> 00:44:31.360
we're measuring mental health

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:34.130
primarily using the general
health questionnaire 12

00:44:34.130 --> 00:44:37.220
which asks about how you've been feeling

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:39.860
in the last two weeks compared to normal.

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:41.200
And then we use the child

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:46.200
and youth resilience measure
to measure resilience.

00:44:46.890 --> 00:44:48.110
And in Jordan, what we find

00:44:48.110 --> 00:44:51.000
that most adolescents are not
psychologically distressed

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:53.640
and that they are emotionally resilient.

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:55.890
So I'm using the general
health questionnaire

00:44:56.894 --> 00:44:59.090
12 to measure psychological distress

00:44:59.090 --> 00:45:02.070
around a third of adolescents had scores

00:45:02.070 --> 00:45:03.870
that demonstrated emotional distress

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:07.950
but this varied across populations.

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:10.830
And so in the Jordan
context, older girls are

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:14.533
11% more likely to be
distressed than older boys.

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:21.580
And in Bangladesh, you know,
rates of emotional distress

00:45:21.580 --> 00:45:25.770
measured by the DHQ 12 are
much are lower around half

00:45:25.770 --> 00:45:28.620
of what's recorded in
Jordan, but there are

00:45:28.620 --> 00:45:30.520
significant differences between older

00:45:30.520 --> 00:45:32.890
and younger adolescents
with, you know, 20%

00:45:32.890 --> 00:45:36.500
of older adolescents reporting,
psychological distress

00:45:36.500 --> 00:45:38.370
compared to 10% of younger adolescents.

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:42.650
And actually the most effected
group in Bangladesh is

00:45:42.650 --> 00:45:46.230
around the older males and particularly

00:45:46.230 --> 00:45:49.870
in the Rohingya camps and
in the qualitative work.

00:45:49.870 --> 00:45:51.880
One of the major concerns that came

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:54.450
out is financial reasons.

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:57.560
And boys, you know, shouldering
the majority of the concern

00:45:57.560 --> 00:46:00.384
about the, you know, work
that they need to do to

00:46:00.384 --> 00:46:01.753
contribute to household incomes.

00:46:05.330 --> 00:46:08.480
So again, kind of thinking
back to the programming

00:46:08.480 --> 00:46:12.120
and what exists and how
we might be able to think

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:16.640
about supporting the psychosocial
wellbeing of adolescents.

00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:20.760
We have looked at among
younger adolescents, the role

00:46:20.760 --> 00:46:23.860
of the Makani programming and
the temporary learning centers

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:28.620
on, you know, resilience and
psychosocial mental health

00:46:28.620 --> 00:46:32.330
as measured by the GHQ 12 and
what we find in both contexts

00:46:32.330 --> 00:46:34.780
and misses particularly
driven by young boys.

00:46:34.780 --> 00:46:37.800
And so these are the results
that I'm showing here is

00:46:37.800 --> 00:46:42.350
that being currently enrolled
in Makani or currently

00:46:42.350 --> 00:46:44.650
attending an NGO program,

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:48.630
reduces the GHQ 12 score

00:46:48.630 --> 00:46:52.330
by about 0.3 points.

00:46:52.330 --> 00:46:55.050
And in Bangladesh, this
translates to about an 11

00:46:55.050 --> 00:46:57.370
percentage point reduction
in the likelihood

00:46:57.370 --> 00:47:01.680
of experiencing psychological
distress, which is, you know

00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:04.675
given that among young
adolescents as a whole around 10%

00:47:04.675 --> 00:47:07.220
of them were experiencing
psychological distress.

00:47:07.220 --> 00:47:09.710
This is quite a large
effect in that, you know

00:47:09.710 --> 00:47:12.150
adolescents that are
engaging in this programming

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:14.770
are having better outcomes

00:47:14.770 --> 00:47:16.400
suggesting that supportive environments

00:47:16.400 --> 00:47:19.133
are crucial in these cases.

00:47:23.488 --> 00:47:26.490
And then finally, I do just
wanna mention, you know, one

00:47:27.860 --> 00:47:29.170
again, coming back to early marriage

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:31.147
and the negative impacts

00:47:31.147 --> 00:47:33.186
that this has on adolescents, as you know

00:47:33.186 --> 00:47:34.019
alongside gender-based violence.

00:47:34.019 --> 00:47:36.530
One of the things that we
found in some recent work I've

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:39.480
been doing on are really married
to the Rohingya population

00:47:40.317 --> 00:47:43.720
is that when you look at PHQ
nine scores, which is another

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:46.630
you know, clinical diagnostic
tool for a moderate

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:50.840
to severe depression, 37% of
our married adolescent girls

00:47:50.840 --> 00:47:54.140
exhibit signs of moderate to
severe depression compared

00:47:54.140 --> 00:47:57.990
to 11% of the older unmarried
girls in the Rohingya camps.

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:02.390
And so, you know, early
marriage, although maybe

00:48:02.390 --> 00:48:03.550
potentially protective

00:48:03.550 --> 00:48:06.623
is causing a fair amount of distress.

00:48:09.590 --> 00:48:13.070
- Jennifer one question before you move on

00:48:13.070 --> 00:48:15.570
so is divorced, socially acceptable

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:18.510
or are there some repercussions
for girls who get divorced?

00:48:18.510 --> 00:48:20.936
Do they go back to live with their parents

00:48:20.936 --> 00:48:23.536
as probably they're sort
of not economically active?

00:48:24.402 --> 00:48:27.010
I can speak to Jordan in that it's

00:48:29.235 --> 00:48:34.160
yes there is stigma associated
with being divorced and yes

00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:38.080
they do typically go back
to live in their homes

00:48:38.080 --> 00:48:40.930
but it has become so common.

00:48:40.930 --> 00:48:44.830
So, you know, kind of, and these many

00:48:44.830 --> 00:48:47.570
of these girls do then go into
marry again fairly quickly.

00:48:47.570 --> 00:48:50.410
So yes, stigma, but at least in Jordan

00:48:50.410 --> 00:48:53.760
has become very common.

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:55.513
And I don't know, Jennifer

00:48:55.513 --> 00:48:56.543
if you can speak to Bangladesh.

00:48:57.750 --> 00:49:02.750
- Yeah in the Rohingya context,
the divorce did not come up

00:49:03.266 --> 00:49:07.250
strongly in the research
on early marriage.

00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:09.271
And my understanding from the context

00:49:09.271 --> 00:49:12.750
it's not socially acceptable

00:49:12.750 --> 00:49:17.750
and so if it's not really
an option in that context.

00:49:22.853 --> 00:49:25.520
- We have about 25 minutes left.

00:49:26.850 --> 00:49:29.020
- Yeah so I've been, I think some

00:49:29.020 --> 00:49:30.450
of this motivation slides I'll be going

00:49:30.450 --> 00:49:32.050
through a little bit quickly

00:49:32.050 --> 00:49:34.630
since we've already kind of
provided that motivation.

00:49:34.630 --> 00:49:36.150
So what I want to talk about here

00:49:36.150 --> 00:49:38.324
and I think I'll focus a little bit

00:49:38.324 --> 00:49:41.153
on mental health and
measurement is some work

00:49:41.153 --> 00:49:44.440
that we're doing using
children's drawings to measure

00:49:44.440 --> 00:49:48.003
as potential measures of mental
health in these contexts.

00:49:49.880 --> 00:49:51.980
So I have a few slides here
sort of motivating mental health

00:49:51.980 --> 00:49:54.330
and adolescents which we
already discussed earlier

00:49:54.330 --> 00:49:58.290
and, you know degrees of mental health

00:49:59.740 --> 00:50:02.300
illness among refugees.

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:04.730
So I'll go through this quickly

00:50:04.730 --> 00:50:07.410
but what I do want to stop and
talk about is that, you know

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:08.870
a big challenge in terms

00:50:08.870 --> 00:50:12.220
of measuring mental health in
these contexts is, you know

00:50:12.220 --> 00:50:14.440
how to assess psychological distress

00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:17.030
for very young adolescents,
particularly when we're talking

00:50:17.030 --> 00:50:20.060
about 10 and 12 year
olds, although, you know

00:50:20.060 --> 00:50:23.000
the GHD 12 has been validated
in multiple populations

00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:26.280
administering these complex
survey instruments in, you know

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:30.270
potentially fragile
contexts can be difficult.

00:50:30.270 --> 00:50:31.980
And especially in cases
where adolescents have

00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:35.250
limited education and are
struggling to understand concepts.

00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:38.010
And so one promising
methodology is children's

00:50:38.010 --> 00:50:42.790
self portraits and free drawings.

00:50:42.790 --> 00:50:45.890
And these have been used in
clinical settings as a gauge of

00:50:45.890 --> 00:50:47.750
of mental health and other contexts.

00:50:47.750 --> 00:50:50.250
And there has been some recent research

00:50:50.250 --> 00:50:51.270
using children's drawings

00:50:51.270 --> 00:50:54.940
for measuring psychological
impacts in field studies.

00:50:54.940 --> 00:50:58.710
And so we're building on this
to look at the feasibility

00:50:58.710 --> 00:51:00.843
in our contexts as well.

00:51:04.040 --> 00:51:07.850
So, you know, the empirical
literature has established link

00:51:07.850 --> 00:51:10.030
between some characteristics
of children's self portraits

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:12.950
and free drawings and elements
of psychological health.

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:14.590
And I'll show some examples

00:51:14.590 --> 00:51:17.140
of what that might look
like in a couple of slides.

00:51:18.460 --> 00:51:20.730
And the nice thing about drawings is

00:51:20.730 --> 00:51:24.250
that if we can associate, you
know, features of drawings

00:51:24.250 --> 00:51:27.900
to mental health, we might
be able to learn things

00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:30.360
about mental health that
would be difficult to obtain

00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:32.373
from direct questioning either

00:51:32.373 --> 00:51:34.060
because adolescents don't
want to respond directly

00:51:34.060 --> 00:51:37.890
or don't understand the
concepts themselves.

00:51:37.890 --> 00:51:40.190
And so what we're doing
with these drawings is

00:51:40.190 --> 00:51:42.770
we basically give the
adolescents a sheet of paper

00:51:42.770 --> 00:51:45.980
and a set of colored pencils
and a flat place to draw

00:51:45.980 --> 00:51:48.910
and we give them simple instructions.

00:51:48.910 --> 00:51:51.730
So we do both free drawings
in the GAGE context.

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:56.730
And then we have from another
data set, self portraits

00:51:56.750 --> 00:51:57.890
but essentially the instructions are, I'd

00:51:57.890 --> 00:51:59.687
like you to draw a picture

00:51:59.687 --> 00:52:00.940
on this piece of paper,
using these pencils

00:52:00.940 --> 00:52:03.480
and feel free to draw whatever
you're thinking about.

00:52:03.480 --> 00:52:06.710
It can be a picture of anything
and they have 15 minutes.

00:52:06.710 --> 00:52:09.460
And if it's a self portrait,
then we just specify that.

00:52:11.754 --> 00:52:13.450
So we have these drawings

00:52:13.450 --> 00:52:17.520
from two datasets among
Syrian refugee families.

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:19.853
And so one was collected in 2016.

00:52:21.419 --> 00:52:24.533
Colleagues at university of San Francisco,

00:52:24.533 --> 00:52:26.646
Bruce Wydick, Raphael, Penn, Leo

00:52:26.646 --> 00:52:29.460
and I'm Stephanie Smith
collaborating with us on this work.

00:52:29.460 --> 00:52:34.400
And then they have data on
children ages five to 12.

00:52:34.400 --> 00:52:36.666
And then we have the GAGE Jordan data

00:52:36.666 --> 00:52:38.310
that we've been talking about
today, focusing particularly

00:52:38.310 --> 00:52:41.410
on the Syrian adolescents, aged 10 to 12

00:52:43.084 --> 00:52:46.350
and for the USF dataset, we have portraits

00:52:46.350 --> 00:52:51.350
and for the GAGE Jordan
dataset, we have free drawing.

00:52:53.350 --> 00:52:57.040
And then we're able to do
surveys and collect information

00:52:57.040 --> 00:52:58.680
on household characteristics

00:52:58.680 --> 00:53:01.160
and governance of origin in Syria

00:53:01.160 --> 00:53:05.050
and pair it with exposure
to trauma, which we

00:53:05.050 --> 00:53:07.690
measure using the Syrian
Revolution Martyr database

00:53:07.690 --> 00:53:09.960
which contains information on sort

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:11.720
of number of martyr deaths

00:53:11.720 --> 00:53:16.720
in governance by, you
know, timing in Syria.

00:53:17.200 --> 00:53:20.640
And so we're able to link
when adolescents traveled

00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:23.610
to Jordan from Syria to sort of the timing

00:53:25.172 --> 00:53:29.640
of martyr deaths in Syria
to measure the exposure

00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:32.240
the extent of exposure
they've had to this violence.

00:53:33.160 --> 00:53:33.993
And then in the future

00:53:33.993 --> 00:53:36.640
we do have drawings and the
GAGE Bangladesh data as well

00:53:37.709 --> 00:53:39.790
although it's currently
still in Bangladesh so

00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:42.090
that we can bring that
into this work as well.

00:53:44.200 --> 00:53:47.550
So just as an example of how features

00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:52.070
of drawings might be used
to measure mental health.

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:55.380
So Tibbets 20 Tibbits
in some previous work

00:53:55.380 --> 00:53:58.450
in 2013 has identified some features

00:53:58.450 --> 00:54:01.270
of drawings that can be linked to PTSD.

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:03.360
And so some of those might be

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:06.600
like in this drawing of the
flag, you know, lack of concern

00:54:06.600 --> 00:54:11.600
with integrating background
into drawing, perhaps, you know

00:54:11.780 --> 00:54:15.440
focus on symbols or memories of trauma.

00:54:15.440 --> 00:54:20.290
So that could be here is a picture it's

00:54:20.290 --> 00:54:23.460
like a man aiming a gun at another person.

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:24.740
And so these are both features

00:54:24.740 --> 00:54:27.020
that have been associated with PTSD.

00:54:27.020 --> 00:54:28.570
And then there's other literature such

00:54:28.570 --> 00:54:33.570
as work by Watson as
old, as far back as 1971

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:37.310
linking features of drawings
to anxiety and depression.

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:39.410
And so one might be drawn in dark colors.

00:54:39.410 --> 00:54:41.510
So this is the black and white picture

00:54:41.510 --> 00:54:43.630
of a hat or with a single color

00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:48.630
of a house, excuse me, or even,
you know, positive features

00:54:48.770 --> 00:54:51.700
of drawings indicating
low anxiety or happiness.

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:55.000
So having, you know,
bright colors, et cetera.

00:54:55.000 --> 00:54:56.830
And so then this is how we've had

00:54:56.830 --> 00:54:59.630
we had these drawings and we're
when we code them according

00:54:59.630 --> 00:55:01.850
to these features and then
are able to classify them

00:55:01.850 --> 00:55:04.620
into indicators of
depression, anxiety, or PTSD

00:55:04.620 --> 00:55:06.920
that we then use as
measures of mental health.

00:55:11.861 --> 00:55:14.230
And then we estimate the relationship

00:55:14.230 --> 00:55:16.710
with these measures of
mental health that we build

00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:19.830
from the indices using a simple regression

00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:22.670
outcome is our index of
anxiety, depression or PTSD.

00:55:22.670 --> 00:55:25.450
And then as our is a vector

00:55:25.450 --> 00:55:27.946
of refugee experiences
that includes exposure

00:55:27.946 --> 00:55:29.353
to violence or simulation.

00:55:29.353 --> 00:55:32.253
And then we control for various
household characteristics.

00:55:34.490 --> 00:55:39.420
And what we find is that
exposure to violence as measured

00:55:39.420 --> 00:55:43.170
by the Syrian modern
database is positively

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:45.210
correlated or indicating higher levels

00:55:45.210 --> 00:55:49.450
of PTSD as measured by
these drawing indexes,

00:55:49.450 --> 00:55:54.340
both in the USF data among
the five to 12 year olds

00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:57.560
in 2016 and in the GAGE Jordan data

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:02.813
measured more recently in 2018 and 2019.

00:56:04.470 --> 00:56:05.390
And then in this data,

00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:08.860
we're also able to measure integration

00:56:08.860 --> 00:56:11.390
of the Syrian refugees
into the Jordan community.

00:56:11.390 --> 00:56:13.520
So those living in host
communities, and we can see

00:56:13.520 --> 00:56:16.610
that adolescents that
have been reintegrated

00:56:16.610 --> 00:56:19.280
into Jordan society, that
this is these supportive

00:56:19.280 --> 00:56:23.000
environments are
associated with reductions

00:56:25.725 --> 00:56:28.310
in experience of PTSD and the USF data.

00:56:28.310 --> 00:56:31.500
And then also reductions
in experience of depression

00:56:31.500 --> 00:56:33.760
and anxiety across both data sets.

00:56:33.760 --> 00:56:36.620
And so what we see is that
these measures of anxiety

00:56:36.620 --> 00:56:41.470
depression, PTSD as indices of
mental health, sorry, indices

00:56:41.470 --> 00:56:45.543
of drawing features are
associated with these violence.

00:56:48.050 --> 00:56:51.240
So then what we wanna do is identify

00:56:51.240 --> 00:56:53.950
in particular features of
these drawings that are

00:56:53.950 --> 00:56:57.710
associated most associated
with exposure to violence.

00:56:57.710 --> 00:56:59.680
And then whether these
same features of drawings

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:01.660
could be associated with
more traditional measures

00:57:01.660 --> 00:57:03.693
of mental health, such as the GHQ 12.

00:57:05.065 --> 00:57:08.797
And we do that using LASSO which is a tool

00:57:13.160 --> 00:57:15.799
to identify basically the
most important correlates

00:57:15.799 --> 00:57:18.773
with an outcome of interest.

00:57:21.560 --> 00:57:25.690
And so we use LASSO to estimate
the following specification

00:57:25.690 --> 00:57:27.960
where we have our outcome in this case

00:57:27.960 --> 00:57:29.410
is now exposure to violence.

00:57:29.410 --> 00:57:31.290
And we're trying to predict the violence

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:35.680
with drawing features, which
we have here in this D feature.

00:57:35.680 --> 00:57:38.410
And we penalize these
drawing features in order to

00:57:38.410 --> 00:57:42.480
identify the most important
drawing features in this case.

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:44.900
And then we have our control

00:57:44.900 --> 00:57:47.963
variables that we don't
penalize characteristics.

00:57:50.510 --> 00:57:53.110
And we estimate this
LASSO separately on each

00:57:53.110 --> 00:57:56.110
of the two datasets, the USF
dataset and the GAGE dataset.

00:57:56.110 --> 00:57:58.055
And what we find is in both cases

00:57:58.055 --> 00:58:02.850
the drawing of aggressive
action figures is retained

00:58:02.850 --> 00:58:05.817
as being highly predictive
of exposure to violence.

00:58:05.817 --> 00:58:08.510
And so this is the most important feature,

00:58:08.510 --> 00:58:11.560
but you can also see other
features that were retained

00:58:11.560 --> 00:58:14.370
through the, through the LASSO estimation

00:58:14.370 --> 00:58:17.579
in each data set as well,
including, you know, shading

00:58:17.579 --> 00:58:21.750
a face of body monster
pictures as also being

00:58:21.750 --> 00:58:24.667
positively correlated with violence.

00:58:24.667 --> 00:58:26.070
And so what we're seeing is

00:58:27.183 --> 00:58:29.580
that there are strong correlations
of these strong features

00:58:29.580 --> 00:58:32.680
as being able to predict higher exposures

00:58:32.680 --> 00:58:34.930
of violence measured
through the Syrian data.

00:58:38.340 --> 00:58:40.630
And then just very quickly

00:58:40.630 --> 00:58:42.420
we then took those features
that were identified

00:58:42.420 --> 00:58:44.530
as predictive of violence,
and can also show

00:58:44.530 --> 00:58:46.870
that they are correlated also

00:58:46.870 --> 00:58:49.150
with signs of psychological distress

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:51.010
which we mainly point out here to say

00:58:51.010 --> 00:58:53.090
that these features are
identifying something

00:58:53.090 --> 00:58:56.730
that is correlated with
mental health as well.

00:58:56.730 --> 00:58:57.830
So that we aren't just talking

00:58:57.830 --> 00:58:59.820
about violence and drawing,

00:58:59.820 --> 00:59:02.413
but also can really link
this back to mental health.

00:59:03.300 --> 00:59:08.243
So I will wrap up there
and hand back to Sarah.

00:59:09.690 --> 00:59:12.423
- Great, thanks Jennifer.

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:16.100
You know, I think there's
a number of questions

00:59:16.100 --> 00:59:18.500
in the chat and I'll make
sure I leave, you know

00:59:19.407 --> 00:59:20.962
five minutes to get back to these.

00:59:20.962 --> 00:59:23.400
I mean, I think mental
health measurement in general

00:59:23.400 --> 00:59:26.700
mental health measurement
among adolescents, you know

00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:29.320
is something where continued
research is needed.

00:59:29.320 --> 00:59:34.320
One thing that, you know, for example

00:59:34.840 --> 00:59:37.929
it seems like some of
the rates of symptoms

00:59:37.929 --> 00:59:40.570
of psychological distress are
lower among these populations

00:59:40.570 --> 00:59:43.780
than typical adolescents in low
and middle income countries.

00:59:43.780 --> 00:59:46.050
And that may be partially
because, you know

00:59:46.050 --> 00:59:48.670
anxiety is such a it's
almost normalized for them.

00:59:48.670 --> 00:59:51.790
And so I think we need to do
a bit more to figure that out.

00:59:51.790 --> 00:59:53.680
And then I think, you know,
when there was a query

00:59:53.680 --> 00:59:56.440
around access to serve as and I think

00:59:56.440 --> 00:59:58.280
you know, yes, there's
stigma around mental health.

00:59:58.280 --> 00:59:59.880
And then even if you identify it

01:00:00.779 --> 01:00:02.054
there's limited access to services.

01:00:02.054 --> 01:00:03.708
I know there are a lot of innovative

01:00:03.708 --> 01:00:06.120
low cost methodologies being explored

01:00:06.120 --> 01:00:08.410
but I really think this is you know

01:00:08.410 --> 01:00:12.020
a place where a lot more
researches is needed.

01:00:12.020 --> 01:00:14.810
Okay so I have about 10 minutes

01:00:14.810 --> 01:00:17.600
so I'm not gonna really
do this part justice

01:00:17.600 --> 01:00:21.680
but given that I think COVID-19
is on the top of our minds

01:00:21.680 --> 01:00:26.060
and particularly given the
massive increases in cases

01:00:26.060 --> 01:00:29.010
in Bangladesh as well
as in Jordan, I think

01:00:29.010 --> 01:00:32.520
is typically salient for these settings.

01:00:32.520 --> 01:00:35.230
I'm gonna skip most of these slides

01:00:35.230 --> 01:00:37.340
but just highlight kind of
a few things that have come

01:00:37.340 --> 01:00:39.920
out of our research.

01:00:39.920 --> 01:00:40.753
Next slide.

01:00:44.070 --> 01:00:45.500
So very quickly, just to, you know,

01:00:45.500 --> 01:00:48.580
for those of you that press
don't work in these contexts

01:00:48.580 --> 01:00:50.910
and haven't been following
what's happening with COVID

01:00:50.910 --> 01:00:53.180
that'd be nice to just provide a graphic

01:00:53.180 --> 01:00:55.460
of kind of the trajectory
of COVID-19 cases

01:00:55.460 --> 01:00:57.920
over time and chemo policy moments

01:00:57.920 --> 01:01:00.440
of most relevance for this conversation is

01:01:00.440 --> 01:01:02.090
that when we conducted the first round

01:01:02.090 --> 01:01:06.000
of COVID data collection back in May

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:09.210
Jordan had one of the most the
strictest lock downs globally

01:01:09.210 --> 01:01:13.280
and cases were very very
low and when we resumed

01:01:13.280 --> 01:01:15.932
our second round of
field work in late 2020,

01:01:15.932 --> 01:01:19.350
early 2021, they had largely relaxed

01:01:19.350 --> 01:01:23.980
those restrictions cases were
increasing pretty dramatically

01:01:23.980 --> 01:01:25.840
and schools tried to reopen briefly

01:01:25.840 --> 01:01:27.293
but then had to shut down.

01:01:28.395 --> 01:01:30.925
And in fact, you know,
last March was actually

01:01:30.925 --> 01:01:32.970
the highest case caseload
in Jordan thus far.

01:01:32.970 --> 01:01:35.060
So there, I think it

01:01:35.060 --> 01:01:37.990
things are starting to
turn, but sort of, you know

01:01:37.990 --> 01:01:40.760
second round of data
collection was taken place

01:01:40.760 --> 01:01:42.349
when there was a fairly high rise.

01:01:42.349 --> 01:01:43.266
Next slide.

01:01:45.060 --> 01:01:47.540
So in Bangladesh, it's I guess, sort of

01:01:47.540 --> 01:01:50.650
a similar story when we
conducted data collection

01:01:50.650 --> 01:01:55.410
in April, May, 2020, the
government had actually

01:01:55.410 --> 01:01:58.130
imposed a very strict lockdown
on Cox's Bazar specifically

01:01:58.130 --> 01:02:01.310
to prevent the spread of
the virus in the camps

01:02:01.310 --> 01:02:05.240
this included closing
all the learning centers

01:02:05.240 --> 01:02:09.653
and sort of other
non-essential NGO programs.

01:02:10.600 --> 01:02:13.408
Data question, as I mentioned,
is still ongoing right now

01:02:13.408 --> 01:02:17.200
but similarly cases are
rising dramatically.

01:02:17.200 --> 01:02:20.551
Bangladesh announced another
lockdown on April 5th

01:02:20.551 --> 01:02:22.530
with sort of a more severe
lockdown set to start

01:02:22.530 --> 01:02:25.720
to tomorrow, complimenting this

01:02:25.720 --> 01:02:27.630
there were actually horrible fires

01:02:27.630 --> 01:02:30.363
in the camps sort of further, you know

01:02:33.090 --> 01:02:34.520
preventing challenges on their lives.

01:02:34.520 --> 01:02:36.260
And up to today, as far as I know

01:02:36.260 --> 01:02:39.917
not a single Rohingya has
received a vaccination.

01:02:39.917 --> 01:02:44.917
Next slide.

01:02:50.862 --> 01:02:52.574
So you know adolescents obviously

01:02:52.574 --> 01:02:55.210
are not really being affected
by the direct health impacts,

01:02:55.210 --> 01:02:58.140
but school closures and the economic

01:02:58.140 --> 01:03:00.910
disruption has been huge.

01:03:00.910 --> 01:03:04.320
We collaboratives to develop a module

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:07.720
just asking for both the
adults and the adolescent

01:03:07.720 --> 01:03:11.020
their perspective on how COVID
has impacted their community

01:03:11.020 --> 01:03:13.900
across a variety of different dimensions.

01:03:13.900 --> 01:03:16.230
And as you can see, almost everyone notes

01:03:16.230 --> 01:03:18.890
that people have lost jobs
and schools have closed.

01:03:18.890 --> 01:03:22.053
And also lots of people
noting increases in violence

01:03:22.053 --> 01:03:25.990
people using shisha and
other types of substances

01:03:26.933 --> 01:03:29.330
people unable to get medical care

01:03:29.330 --> 01:03:32.290
and then issues around
self harm and suicide.

01:03:32.290 --> 01:03:34.280
And this has also come out
in the qualitative work.

01:03:34.280 --> 01:03:36.870
So this just highlights how dramatic this

01:03:36.870 --> 01:03:39.540
the shock has been as
perceived by these households.

01:03:39.540 --> 01:03:40.373
Next slide.

01:03:42.720 --> 01:03:44.050
Similarly in Bangladesh.

01:03:44.050 --> 01:03:46.240
So here, these are from
the adult's perspective

01:03:46.240 --> 01:03:47.520
comparing and coast and camp.

01:03:47.520 --> 01:03:49.200
And once again, you know

01:03:49.200 --> 01:03:51.920
most households have effect had some sort

01:03:52.973 --> 01:03:56.700
of employment or income
loss schools have closed

01:03:56.700 --> 01:03:59.790
and people are perceiving,
you know, increased anxiety

01:03:59.790 --> 01:04:03.423
increased violence, food
insecurity, et cetera.

01:04:04.380 --> 01:04:09.000
So while the direct health
effects have not been

01:04:10.070 --> 01:04:11.580
at least to date, perhaps as large

01:04:11.580 --> 01:04:14.708
as they have been in
places like the US you know

01:04:14.708 --> 01:04:16.991
the lockdowns in these
contexts have sort of

01:04:16.991 --> 01:04:18.460
massively disrupted these people's lives.

01:04:18.460 --> 01:04:19.293
Next slide..

01:04:22.160 --> 01:04:25.960
So I'm gonna have to just
focus on a few key results.

01:04:25.960 --> 01:04:28.740
This is the first one, which
is around food insecurity.

01:04:28.740 --> 01:04:31.760
So we asked the adolescents
how many meals they had

01:04:31.760 --> 01:04:33.910
with animal protein yesterday.

01:04:33.910 --> 01:04:37.540
This also includes some
data from our other sites

01:04:37.540 --> 01:04:39.930
and I guess, perhaps not
surprisingly given the magnitude

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:42.930
of the economic shock,
you can see that when

01:04:42.930 --> 01:04:44.510
you compare baseline.

01:04:44.510 --> 01:04:48.050
So this is pre COVID to
COVID round one, which is

01:04:48.050 --> 01:04:51.630
the early pandemic and to
Jordan and COVID round two

01:04:51.630 --> 01:04:54.777
you can see that the
number of meals with animal

01:04:54.777 --> 01:04:57.330
protein dropped
substantially and in Jordan,

01:04:57.330 --> 01:05:00.500
we still haven't seen
any sign of recovery.

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:04.070
So the economic effects
and then its translation

01:05:04.070 --> 01:05:07.054
into food insecurity,
which has sort of much

01:05:07.054 --> 01:05:08.610
and I were talking in the beginning.

01:05:08.610 --> 01:05:10.690
I think the nutrition is
particularly important

01:05:10.690 --> 01:05:11.900
for adolescent growth.

01:05:11.900 --> 01:05:14.710
And so this is something
to be concerned about.

01:05:14.710 --> 01:05:15.543
Next slide

01:05:17.580 --> 01:05:20.170
In Jordan we can also further look at

01:05:20.170 --> 01:05:21.600
differences in experiences

01:05:21.600 --> 01:05:25.770
between host informal tented,
settlements, and camps here.

01:05:25.770 --> 01:05:27.640
We're looking at whether
adolescents experienced hunger

01:05:27.640 --> 01:05:29.090
in the last four weeks.

01:05:29.090 --> 01:05:32.099
So again, you see these sort of increases

01:05:32.099 --> 01:05:33.190
at COVID are one and

01:05:33.190 --> 01:05:38.190
in both hosts and its
really no sign of recovery

01:05:38.320 --> 01:05:39.620
but in camps you can see

01:05:39.620 --> 01:05:43.960
that it does look like there
has been some improvement

01:05:43.960 --> 01:05:47.930
and this points to the extensive
access to social protection

01:05:47.930 --> 01:05:50.010
that adolescents have in camps that

01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:55.010
is not there in hosts in ICF communities.

01:05:55.700 --> 01:05:56.533
Next slide.

01:06:00.030 --> 01:06:02.380
I'm not gonna do this justice,
but I really just want to

01:06:03.646 --> 01:06:05.970
quickly note that Jordan has
actually done a fantastic job

01:06:05.970 --> 01:06:10.970
relative to other countries
in terms of quickly pivoting

01:06:11.280 --> 01:06:15.767
to online learning and given
the access to technology

01:06:16.890 --> 01:06:21.707
et cetera, you can see that
many, most kids are able to

01:06:22.730 --> 01:06:25.840
engage in some learning
through this ministry

01:06:25.840 --> 01:06:29.490
of education program, even so you can see

01:06:29.490 --> 01:06:31.740
through these qualitative
quotes that, you know

01:06:31.740 --> 01:06:34.660
it's unclear how much learning
is actually happening.

01:06:34.660 --> 01:06:37.930
And I think there's a lot of
concern about what happens when

01:06:37.930 --> 01:06:40.660
schools reopen after
this sort of last year

01:06:40.660 --> 01:06:41.520
of education.

01:06:41.520 --> 01:06:43.780
But you know, again,
relative to other contexts,

01:06:43.780 --> 01:06:47.220
Jordan has you know, I
think is exemplar in terms

01:06:47.220 --> 01:06:50.380
of ability to pivot to online learning.

01:06:50.380 --> 01:06:53.913
And I think benefits from
it's wide access to internet.

01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:55.593
Next slide.

01:06:57.570 --> 01:07:00.960
And Bangladesh you know,
it's a bit of the opposite.

01:07:00.960 --> 01:07:05.960
So I'll just focus on
the purple shape, only 6%

01:07:06.124 --> 01:07:08.630
of Bangladeshi's report
using any kind of media to

01:07:08.630 --> 01:07:09.910
continue learning.

01:07:09.910 --> 01:07:13.270
And there's literally only
one adolescent in our sample.

01:07:13.270 --> 01:07:14.850
Who's had any sort of have access

01:07:14.850 --> 01:07:18.080
to learning for media during the pandemic.

01:07:18.080 --> 01:07:20.980
So this, you know, I think

01:07:20.980 --> 01:07:24.020
highlights the fact
that the pandemic really

01:07:24.020 --> 01:07:26.500
is going to exacerbate these
pre-existing inequalities

01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:28.520
both across and within countries.

01:07:28.520 --> 01:07:30.390
And we're going to need
to think of a programming.

01:07:30.390 --> 01:07:32.790
That's gonna be
particularly focus on that.

01:07:32.790 --> 01:07:33.623
Next slide.

01:07:36.800 --> 01:07:40.400
Okay I guess quickly go through
a couple of final themes.

01:07:40.400 --> 01:07:42.140
So, you know, we think a lot about

01:07:42.140 --> 01:07:46.850
about school closures and how
that has isolated adolescents.

01:07:46.850 --> 01:07:48.360
You know, Mr. Flick was
saying he had to come back

01:07:48.360 --> 01:07:50.790
from the beach because
his son wanted to interact

01:07:50.790 --> 01:07:53.190
with friends again and you know

01:07:53.190 --> 01:07:55.860
social connectedness is
actually extremely important

01:07:55.860 --> 01:07:58.650
for adolescents and
isolation is associated

01:07:58.650 --> 01:08:01.410
with mental health,
anxiety and other outcomes.

01:08:01.410 --> 01:08:04.670
So he did good by his son.

01:08:04.670 --> 01:08:07.490
So this highlights
basically how isolated many

01:08:07.490 --> 01:08:10.810
of these adolescents are
prior to the pandemic.

01:08:10.810 --> 01:08:12.990
Pretty much everyone was
interacting with a friend

01:08:12.990 --> 01:08:16.940
or non household member at
least once in the seven days.

01:08:16.940 --> 01:08:21.590
But as you can see in
Jordan only about 30%

01:08:21.590 --> 01:08:24.430
of both boys and girls were interacting

01:08:24.430 --> 01:08:27.470
with friends at all, some
signs of recovery, right?

01:08:27.470 --> 01:08:29.170
So we see by COVID round two

01:08:29.170 --> 01:08:31.410
those numbers have
increased, but importantly

01:08:31.410 --> 01:08:33.810
they increase a lot more
for boys than girls.

01:08:33.810 --> 01:08:37.180
So girls are still are sort
of increasingly disadvantaged

01:08:37.180 --> 01:08:39.080
in terms of these social connections.

01:08:39.080 --> 01:08:40.376
And then looking

01:08:40.376 --> 01:08:42.180
at the cost is our panel
survey for round one.

01:08:42.180 --> 01:08:44.270
This really just highlights
the gender discrepancies

01:08:44.270 --> 01:08:46.410
in social connectedness and how

01:08:47.400 --> 01:08:50.610
how much girls have been
sort of socially isolated

01:08:50.610 --> 01:08:51.830
from their networks.

01:08:51.830 --> 01:08:54.750
And I think, you know,
alongside school return

01:08:54.750 --> 01:08:58.210
we need to think about how we re-establish

01:09:00.189 --> 01:09:02.100
the social connections.

01:09:02.100 --> 01:09:02.933
Next slide,

01:09:05.200 --> 01:09:06.033
Next slide.

01:09:09.310 --> 01:09:10.143
Next slide.

01:09:13.680 --> 01:09:15.300
So I very quickly on this.

01:09:15.300 --> 01:09:17.030
So, you know, it sounds like

01:09:17.030 --> 01:09:18.930
from the comments that a lot
of you were interested in

01:09:18.930 --> 01:09:21.250
in sort of measurement issues as well.

01:09:21.250 --> 01:09:24.130
So, you know, and so one
challenge we face inhibiting

01:09:24.130 --> 01:09:25.770
to a virtual survey is we have a lot

01:09:25.770 --> 01:09:30.460
of sensitive questions
and very rigorous policies

01:09:30.460 --> 01:09:33.300
around connecting adolescents to services.

01:09:33.300 --> 01:09:36.240
If they, you know, self-report, you know

01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:38.690
whether it's exposure to
violence or something else.

01:09:38.690 --> 01:09:41.260
And so we didn't feel
that we could adequately

01:09:41.260 --> 01:09:43.040
protect their privacy or connect them

01:09:43.040 --> 01:09:46.000
to services in the context
of the COVID-19 pandemic.

01:09:46.000 --> 01:09:49.290
So instead of acting directly
about exposure to violence

01:09:49.290 --> 01:09:51.640
we developed a series of
vignettes that basically asked

01:09:51.640 --> 01:09:53.510
about whether a specific type

01:09:53.510 --> 01:09:56.240
of violence was a challenge
for adolescents like them

01:09:56.240 --> 01:09:58.700
and whether they thought it
had increased since COVID.

01:09:58.700 --> 01:09:59.880
And so this kind of gives you a sense

01:09:59.880 --> 01:10:03.830
of the types of questions
we're asking and, you know,

01:10:03.830 --> 01:10:07.490
the proportion of the
population that was saying

01:10:07.490 --> 01:10:10.113
that this was a challenge
that it increased

01:10:10.113 --> 01:10:11.380
since COVID as a way to get a sense

01:10:12.499 --> 01:10:15.080
of how the pandemic is affecting
violence, as well as things

01:10:15.080 --> 01:10:18.903
like access to feminine hygiene products.

01:10:19.890 --> 01:10:20.723
Next slide,

01:10:23.030 --> 01:10:24.730
and just the same sort of set

01:10:24.730 --> 01:10:26.370
of vignettes for, for Bangladesh.

01:10:26.370 --> 01:10:29.920
And I think in general, you
know, highlighting violence

01:10:29.920 --> 01:10:31.592
is pervasive and that

01:10:31.592 --> 01:10:35.260
there has been some increases
since since COVID-19.

01:10:35.260 --> 01:10:36.093
Next slide.

01:10:41.530 --> 01:10:44.390
So I'll just end with this finding

01:10:44.390 --> 01:10:48.320
before opening it up
for any final questions.

01:10:48.320 --> 01:10:49.400
So, you know, there's a lot

01:10:49.400 --> 01:10:52.120
of concern around whether
COVID-19 is going to

01:10:52.120 --> 01:10:54.820
exacerbate child marriage, right?

01:10:54.820 --> 01:10:58.530
So I think, you know, we've
highlighted the vulnerabilities

01:10:58.530 --> 01:11:00.320
of girls who are already married

01:11:00.320 --> 01:11:03.780
and our COVID-19 findings indicate that

01:11:03.780 --> 01:11:05.430
they have been disproportionately affected

01:11:05.430 --> 01:11:06.930
along many dimensions

01:11:06.930 --> 01:11:09.580
but obviously there's also
concerned about currently

01:11:09.580 --> 01:11:12.440
non-married girls and
whether this is going to

01:11:12.440 --> 01:11:14.480
sort of accelerate their
transition to marriage.

01:11:14.480 --> 01:11:16.433
Well, I think it's too early

01:11:16.433 --> 01:11:18.692
in our quantitative
data to be able to look

01:11:18.692 --> 01:11:20.480
at changes in marriage rates.

01:11:20.480 --> 01:11:23.060
An interesting dichotomy
appeared when we asked them

01:11:23.060 --> 01:11:25.340
whether they thought their
pressure to marry had decreased

01:11:25.340 --> 01:11:27.690
or whether they worried
about marrying earlier.

01:11:28.640 --> 01:11:31.290
We actually see that
quite a few adolescents

01:11:31.290 --> 01:11:33.360
actually believe that
the pressure to marry

01:11:33.360 --> 01:11:38.360
has decreased in both
Bangladesh and Jordan.

01:11:38.860 --> 01:11:40.850
This could be for multiple reasons.

01:11:40.850 --> 01:11:44.110
One is marriage brings a
financial burden and many

01:11:44.110 --> 01:11:46.616
of these households simply
can't afford that right now

01:11:46.616 --> 01:11:50.160
second marriage comes
with a ceremony and lots

01:11:50.160 --> 01:11:54.450
of people gathering and COVID
doesn't allow that to happen.

01:11:54.450 --> 01:11:56.580
And then third, you know,
sort of getting back to some

01:11:56.580 --> 01:11:59.470
of the conversation Jennifer
and Mushfiq was having

01:11:59.470 --> 01:12:02.050
as I think given that
girls are stuck at home

01:12:02.050 --> 01:12:04.870
there's less concern about their sort

01:12:04.870 --> 01:12:09.870
of sexual purity and their
exposure to kind of violence

01:12:10.290 --> 01:12:11.197
outside of the household.

01:12:11.197 --> 01:12:15.150
And so perhaps also a
little bit less rush to

01:12:15.150 --> 01:12:18.240
get them married and have kids, et cetera.

01:12:18.240 --> 01:12:21.530
On the other hand, you know,
we do have a other subset

01:12:21.530 --> 01:12:24.920
of the population who are
concerned about marrying earlier.

01:12:24.920 --> 01:12:26.430
And so it'll be, you know, one

01:12:26.430 --> 01:12:28.640
of the beauties about
this data being embedded

01:12:28.640 --> 01:12:30.530
in longitudinal work
is we'll be able to see

01:12:30.530 --> 01:12:32.790
over time what actually happened

01:12:32.790 --> 01:12:35.920
with marriage rates as a result of COVID

01:12:35.920 --> 01:12:38.810
and whether, you know, this

01:12:38.810 --> 01:12:40.850
we are actually seeing this dichotomy

01:12:40.850 --> 01:12:42.520
and this actually comes out

01:12:42.520 --> 01:12:45.080
in a lot of our COVID data is, you know

01:12:45.080 --> 01:12:46.410
certain households saying
they're getting a lot

01:12:46.410 --> 01:12:48.480
more support others saying
they're getting a lot less.

01:12:48.480 --> 01:12:52.220
And so exploring those
dichotomies in attitudes

01:12:52.220 --> 01:12:53.290
I think will be important

01:12:53.290 --> 01:12:56.770
in looking at sort of post COVID recovery.

01:12:56.770 --> 01:12:57.603
Next slide.

01:12:59.040 --> 01:13:00.793
And if you can just go to the conclusions.

01:13:06.945 --> 01:13:08.880
Right? So I basically, we highlighted some

01:13:08.880 --> 01:13:11.190
of the vulnerabilities is
adolescents look at risk

01:13:11.190 --> 01:13:14.670
and protective factors and
explored the impact of COVID.

01:13:14.670 --> 01:13:17.090
You know, I think we
have a lot more to say

01:13:17.090 --> 01:13:18.920
but hopefully this has
given you a broad sense

01:13:18.920 --> 01:13:21.630
of what our research is about.

01:13:21.630 --> 01:13:22.463
Next slide.

01:13:25.200 --> 01:13:26.920
As I mentioned, you know,
this as long as, you know

01:13:26.920 --> 01:13:29.200
work that will continue through 2024.

01:13:29.200 --> 01:13:31.700
So we have one to two more
rounds in each context

01:13:31.700 --> 01:13:34.760
to both look at post COVID
recovery, improve measures

01:13:34.760 --> 01:13:38.170
and understand the
transitions to adulthood.

01:13:38.170 --> 01:13:41.540
We're very deeply engaged
with policy makers globally

01:13:41.540 --> 01:13:43.610
to ensure that research
findings inform policy

01:13:43.610 --> 01:13:47.370
and programming, all data
will be publicly archived.

01:13:47.370 --> 01:13:49.150
So, you know, this, as we go,

01:13:49.150 --> 01:13:51.661
this data will be available for others.

01:13:51.661 --> 01:13:54.280
And we do really welcome
broad multidisciplinary

01:13:54.280 --> 01:13:56.340
